    <div class="content">
        <div class="content-inner">
            <section aria-labelledby="h-see">
                <h2 id="h-see">Time you can see</h2>
                <p>If your brain doesn&rsquo;t <em>feel</em> minutes passing, a very ADHD thing called <strong>time blindness</strong>, a countdown is just a number that surprises you later. So this timer shows time three ways at once:</p>
                <ul class="plain">
                    <li><strong>A bar that fills</strong> across the top as your sprint runs.</li>
                    <li><strong>A tab title that counts down</strong>, so you can see it from any other tab.</li>
                    <li><strong>The clock time you&rsquo;ll finish</strong>, &ldquo;done around 3:42&rdquo;. &ldquo;25 minutes&rdquo; is abstract; &ldquo;3:42&rdquo; is something you can hold onto.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="h-how">
                <h2 id="h-how">How it works</h2>
                <div class="step-grid">
                    <div class="how-step"><div class="n" aria-hidden="true">1</div><h3>Name one task</h3><p>Type a single task under the timer. Not a list, one thing. That&rsquo;s the whole plan.</p></div>
                    <div class="how-step"><div class="n" aria-hidden="true">2</div><h3>Pick a length</h3><p>10, 15, 25, or 45 minutes. If starting feels impossible, choose tiny. Ten focused minutes beats zero perfect ones.</p></div>
                    <div class="how-step"><div class="n" aria-hidden="true">3</div><h3>Focus, then rest</h3><p>Work until the chime, then take a real break, stand up, stretch, look out a window. Then go again.</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section aria-labelledby="h-brains">
                <h2 id="h-brains">Made for ADHD brains (and every other kind)</h2>
                <p>Every choice here is deliberate:</p>
                <ul class="plain">
                    <li><strong>Starting is the hard part</strong>, so the presets go down to 10 minutes and the start button is the biggest thing on the page.</li>
                    <li><strong>Decisions are distractions</strong>, so there&rsquo;s one task line and one button, not a project manager.</li>
                    <li><strong>Nothing relies on color alone.</strong> Each mode has its own background color, picked to stay distinct in every common type of color vision, and it always comes with an icon and a word, with states written out in plain language.</li>
                    <li><strong>Motion is optional.</strong> If your system asks for reduced motion, transitions stop and everything holds still.</li>
                    <li><strong>Keyboard first.</strong> Space starts and pauses, R resets, S skips, and you can turn the shortcuts off in Settings.</li>
                    <li><strong>Private by default.</strong> No account, no tracking, your settings and task live in your browser and nowhere else.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

